---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import Cta from '../components/Cta.astro';
import LinkRail from '../components/LinkRail.astro';

const path = '/trusted-partners/';

/* LOCAL REFERRALS — REBUILT FROM THE LEGACY SITE, 4 Sep 2026.
 *
 * WHERE THIS CAME BACK FROM, because it matters. The rebuild's redirect map
 * pointed /trusted-partners/ at /network/ on the assumption that it was
 * brand-network content. It was not. The legacy WordPress site is still live
 * at the production domain while this rebuild sits on a preview URL, so the
 * page could simply be fetched and read, and what it actually carried was a
 * referral list: one vetted local company, the services we do not offer, and
 * a stated bar for being listed. That is a genuinely useful page and it was
 * about to be thrown away on a guess. Read the old page before redirecting
 * it.
 *
 * THE REDIRECT IS GONE, NOT REPOINTED. `trusted-partners` was removed from
 * the legacy map in src/data/legacy-urls.json because this URL is now a real
 * page, and scripts/redirects.mjs fails the build if a redirect source
 * shadows a built page. Same URL as the legacy site, so nothing that links to
 * it from outside breaks.
 *
 * WHAT IS VERIFIED HERE AND WHAT IS NOT. Every detail about King of Kings
 * below was read off their own website on 4 Sep 2026 — the owner's name and
 * title, the phone number, and each service named — rather than copied from
 * our old page. Two of those had drifted in the legacy copy's phrasing. The
 * one claim that is ours rather than theirs is the recommendation itself, and
 * it is stated as ours.
 *
 * WHAT MUST NEVER BE ADDED HERE. A paid listing. The page states that nobody
 * pays to be on it, which turns every future addition into a promise being
 * kept or broken. If that ever changes, the sentence comes out first.
 *
 * WHY COASTAL K9 IS NOT IN THE LOCAL LIST, added 8 Sep 2026 on the owner's
 * request. The page sets four conditions for being listed and the second is
 * "local — actually based in and serving Whatcom or Skagit County, not a
 * national call center routing leads". Coastal K9 & Pest Solutions is in
 * Aiken, South Carolina, some two and a half thousand miles from here, and it
 * is a full-service pest control company in its own right, which also fails
 * the third condition on a plain reading.
 *
 * Dropping it in among the local referrals would have broken the page's own
 * stated bar in the exact place the page invites a reader to check it, which
 * is worse than not listing anybody. So it goes in a section of its own, and
 * that section says where they are and why they are here: the referral is
 * specifically for canine detection, which nobody in this county offers and
 * which they run nationwide for food-processing and agricultural sites. That
 * is a real service, genuinely complementary, and honestly labeled.
 *
 * If the owner wants them presented as a local partner instead, the bar
 * further down the page has to change first, and it should not.
 *
 * JORGE IS NOT A REFERRAL EITHER and is filed separately for the same kind of
 * reason. He consults TO this company; he is not somebody we send customers
 * to. See OWNER DECISION #3 in business.ts — naming him is permitted,
 * describing him as staff is not, and the section here repeats neither the
 * wording nor the claims of the fuller treatment on /about/.
 *
 * WHY IT IS FILED AS A UTILITY PAGE in scripts/harness.mjs. The M1 content
 * floor is 3,000 words and this page cannot honestly reach it: it exists to
 * hand a reader somebody else's phone number and get out of the way, and
 * padding a referral to three thousand words would make it worse at the only
 * job it has. Same class as /contact/ and /network/, and declared in the open
 * in the harness rather than applied quietly.
 */

/* 40–60 words on the answer (src/lib/seo.ts checkAnswer) — recount on edit.
   This is 52. */
const answer =
  'There are jobs we do not do. Rather than shrug when a customer asks, we send them to somebody we would use ourselves, and nobody pays to be listed. The local list is short by design; separate sections name an out-of-state detection specialist and the entomologist we consult.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Do companies pay to be listed on this page?',
    a: 'No. There is no fee, no commission and no referral arrangement. The list is short for exactly that reason — it is made up of businesses we already call when a job falls outside pest control, not businesses that asked to be here.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does Sasquatch Pest Control do window or gutter cleaning?',
    a: 'No. We handle the pests that turn up in gutters, on glass and under eaves — rodents using a debris-packed gutter as a route to the roofline, wasp nests in soffits, spider webbing across window frames — but the cleaning itself is a different trade with different equipment and different insurance. We refer that work out.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I check a company you refer me to?',
    a: 'The same way you should check us. Washington State publishes a free contractor and business verification tool through the Department of Labor & Industries that shows registration status, workers\' compensation coverage and any citations. It takes a couple of minutes and it is worth doing before anybody works on your property.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I get my business listed here?',
    a: 'Get in touch, but read the bar first. A company has to be licensed and insured for the work it does in Washington, actually based in and serving Whatcom or Skagit County, offering something we do not offer rather than competing with us, and a business we would hire at our own homes. Most inquiries fail the last one.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens if a listed company stops meeting that standard?',
    a: 'They come off the page. That is the whole reason this list is short and the reason it is worth anything — a referral we would not stand behind is worse for us than no referral at all, because the customer heard the recommendation from us.',
  },
];

const rails = [
  { href: '/services/', label: 'Everything we do handle' },
  { href: '/services/exclusion-and-repairs/', label: 'Pest exclusion & repairs' },
  { href: '/awards/', label: 'Awards, press and how to verify a company' },
  { href: '/about/', label: 'Our licenses and our crew' },
  { href: '/contact/', label: 'Get in touch' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  path={path}
  title="Trusted Partners & Local Referrals | Sasquatch Pest"
  description="The local companies we send customers to for work Sasquatch Pest Control does not do. Nobody pays to be listed, and the bar for getting on the page is set out here."
  kind="page"
  faqs={faqs}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Trusted partners', path },
  ]}
>
  <h1>Local referrals: who we send people to</h1>
  <AnswerBox text={answer} />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      There are jobs we do not do. When a customer asks about one, we would
      rather send them to somebody good than shrug.
    </p>
    <p>
      The companies on this page are local, licensed and insured, and they do
      work we would be comfortable having done at our own homes.{' '}
      <b>Nobody pays to be listed here.</b> There is no fee, no commission and
      no arrangement of any kind — these are simply the businesses we call when
      a job falls outside what Sasquatch Pest Control does. If a company on
      this page ever stops meeting that standard, it comes off.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why the local list is one name long rather than twenty. A page of
      twenty referrals is a directory, and a directory nobody vetted is worth
      nothing to the person reading it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two other things are named further down and neither is a local referral,
      so neither sits in that list. One is a canine detection specialist based
      out of state. The other is the entomologist this company consults. Both
      are labeled for what they are.
    </p>

    <h2>King of Kings Window Cleaning</h2>
    <p>
      <b>Exterior cleaning and window care · Bellingham, WA · serving Whatcom
      and Skagit County</b>
    </p>
    <p>
      King of Kings Window Cleaning is a locally owned exterior cleaning
      company based in Bellingham, founded and run by Randy Fee. Like us, it is
      run from here rather than dispatched from a franchise desk in another
      state.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>What they handle:</b> residential and commercial window cleaning,
      gutter cleaning and downspout clearing, roof cleaning, moss removal,
      pressure washing, house washing and solar panel cleaning.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Why we send people to them.</b> Pest work and exterior cleaning run
      into each other constantly, and the overlap is not incidental. Spider
      webbing and droppings across glass and window frames. Wasp nests tucked
      up under eaves and soffits. Gutters packed with needles and leaf litter,
      which hold moisture against the fascia and give rodents a covered highway
      to the roofline — one of the most common contributing conditions we find
      on a rodent job in this county, and one we can point at but not fix.
    </p>
    <p>
      We handle the pests. The cleanup, and the conditions that keep inviting
      them back, are a different trade. King of Kings does that work properly.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://www.kingofkingswindowcleaning.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">
        kingofkingswindowcleaning.com
      </a>{' '}
      · <a href="tel:+13603036806">(360) 303-6806</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Canine detection: Coastal K9 &amp; Pest Solutions</h2>
    <p>
      <b>K9 bed bug and rodent detection · Aiken, South Carolina · nationwide
      detection for food processing and agriculture</b>
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>They are not local, and that is why they are in a section of their
      own.</b> Everything above this heading is a Whatcom or Skagit County
      business we would call for a job at our own house. Coastal K9 is two and
      a half thousand miles away. Listing them among the local referrals would
      break the standard set out further down this page, in the exact place we
      invite you to hold us to it.
    </p>
    <p>
      What they do that nobody here does is work detection dogs. Coastal K9 is
      run by Kimberly Camera, a FEMA-trained urban search and rescue handler,
      and the teams carry NESDCA certification — the National Entomology Scent
      Detection Canine Association, which is the body that certifies bed bug
      detection dogs. They run canine bed bug detection, canine rodent
      detection, and a nationwide rodent detection service aimed at
      food-processing and agricultural facilities.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Why that is worth a Whatcom County reader knowing about.</b> A trained
      dog answers a question a person cannot answer reliably: which rooms, and
      is it actually still active. In a hotel, an apartment block or a care
      facility, sweeping forty units by eye is slow and it misses low-level
      activity; a canine sweep narrows treatment to the rooms that need it and
      leaves the rest alone. For a food-processing plant, canine rodent
      detection finds harborage inside structures where visual inspection
      cannot reach.
    </p>
    <p>
      This county has dairies, berry operations, cold storage and food
      manufacturing, and none of it has a local canine option. If your problem
      is one where detection would genuinely change the treatment, that is a
      referral worth making even though it means somebody traveling.
    </p>
    <p>
      To be plain about the limits: we have not worked alongside them on a job
      in Whatcom County, they are not licensed in Washington, and what is
      described above is what their own materials state rather than something
      we have watched. Weigh it accordingly.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://ck9ps.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">ck9ps.com</a>
    </p>

    <h2>The entomologist we consult</h2>
    <p>
      <b>Jorge Bedoya, Associate Certified Entomologist</b>
    </p>
    <p>
      This one is the other way round from everything else on the page. Jorge
      is not somebody we send you to — he is somebody we go to. He holds the
      Associate Certified Entomologist credential from the Entomological
      Society of America and reviews species identification and treatment
      protocols for this company.
    </p>
    <p>
      He is a consultant rather than a member of our staff, which means he does
      not run routes, attend jobs or supervise technicians. And ACE is a
      professional certification awarded by a scientific society, not a
      Washington pesticide license — a different credential doing a different
      job from the WSDA licenses our technicians hold.
    </p>
    <p>
      He is named here because knowing who a company asks when it is not sure
      is worth as much as knowing who turns up at the door. The fuller account,
      including what the arrangement is actually for, is on our{' '}
      <a href="/about/">about page</a> alongside the license numbers.
    </p>

    <h2>Services we do not provide</h2>
    <p>
      So there is no confusion about where our work stops, here is what
      Sasquatch Pest Control does not do — and who we would point you to
      instead.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Window cleaning, gutter cleaning, roof cleaning and moss removal —
        King of Kings Window Cleaning
      </li>
      <li>
        Pressure washing, house washing and solar panel cleaning — King of
        Kings Window Cleaning
      </li>
      <li>
        Canine bed bug or rodent detection — we have no detection dog and no
        handler, and nobody in this county does. See Coastal K9 above.
      </li>
      <li>
        Wood-destroying organism reports for a real estate transaction — that
        requires a Washington structural pest inspector license, which is a
        separate credential from the pesticide applicator licenses we hold. Ask
        your lender or agent for an inspector; we can treat what one finds.
      </li>
      <li>
        Structural repair beyond exclusion work — we seal, screen, flash and
        replace the materials rodents and insects come through, but framing,
        roofing and siding replacement are a contractor's job.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For everything we do handle, see our{' '}
      <a href="/services/">pest control services</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Check anybody before you hire them, including us</h2>
    <p>
      A referral from us is a starting point, not a substitute for looking
      something up. Washington State publishes a free verification tool through
      the Department of Labor &amp; Industries covering business registration,
      workers' compensation coverage and citation history — it needs no account
      and takes about two minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our <a href="/awards/">awards and recognition page</a> sets out how to
      use that tool and the Department of Agriculture's license search, with
      our own numbers to try them on. Run them on us first if you like. That is
      rather the point of publishing them.
    </p>

    <h2>Want to be listed here?</h2>
    <p>
      We are open to adding partners, and the bar is deliberately high. To be
      listed, a company needs to be:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Licensed and insured for the work they do in Washington State</li>
      <li>
        Local — actually based in and serving Whatcom or Skagit County, not a
        national call center routing leads
      </li>
      <li>
        Complementary, not competing — a service Sasquatch Pest Control does
        not offer
      </li>
      <li>
        Somebody we would hire ourselves, with work and customer care we are
        willing to put our name behind
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Think your business fits? <a href="/contact/">Get in touch</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Faq items={faqs} />

  <LinkRail heading="Related" links={rails} />

  <Cta market="Whatcom County" />
</BaseLayout>
